"125_winui_demotool.mst — WG14b's proof, and the shape every later tool copies.

 A tool window is three things and no more: a title, a block that ANSWERS A
 SPEC, and a block that HANDLES AN EVENT. There is no window code here, no
 control code, no layout code and no teardown code — all of that is
 `WinToolWindow`'s, which is the entire point of having built it.

 IT SHOWS ITS OWN COUNTERS, deliberately. `patches` and `rebuilds` are on
 screen because that is what makes the claim checkable by looking: press the
 button and the caption changes while `rebuilds` stays exactly where it was.
 If the id strategy ever stops working, this window says so in its own face
 instead of merely flickering.

 THE STATE IS THE TOOL'S OWN, held in class variables, which is why closing
 the window can HIDE rather than destroy and reopening is instant."

Object subclass: WinDemoTool [
    <classVars: Clicks Note Loud>

    WinDemoTool class >> clicks ^ <Integer> [ ^Clicks isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ Clicks ] ]
    WinDemoTool class >> note ^ <String> [ ^Note isNil ifTrue: [ 'edit me' ] ifFalse: [ Note ] ]
    WinDemoTool class >> loud ^ <Boolean> [ ^Loud == true ]

    "── the spec ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

     EVERY NODE IS NAMED. That is the whole discipline: a named tree diffs to
     `set this caption` instead of `build this window again`, and the counters
     below are what prove it did."

    WinDemoTool class >> spec ^ <WinNode> [
        ^WinSpec window: 'Declarative Demo' id: #demoWin body: (WinSpec stack: {
            WinSpec heading: 'A window made of data' id: #head.
            WinSpec text: 'Nothing here was placed by hand.' id: #blurb.
            WinSpec divider: #rule.

            WinSpec card: 'State' children: {
                WinSpec text: 'clicks: ' , self clicks printString id: #clicks.
                WinSpec text: 'note: ' , self note id: #noteEcho.
                WinSpec text: 'loud: ' , self loud printString id: #loudEcho
            } id: #stateCard.

            WinSpec card: 'Do something' children: {
                WinSpec row: {
                    WinSpec button: 'Click me' event: #bump id: #bump.
                    WinSpec button: 'Reset' event: #reset id: #reset
                } id: #buttons.
                WinSpec input: 'Note' value: self note event: #note id: #noteField.
                WinSpec checkbox: 'Loud' checked: self loud event: #loud id: #loudBox
            } id: #doCard.

            WinSpec card: 'Reconciliation' children: {
                WinSpec text: 'patches:  ' , WinToolWindow patchCount printString
                        id: #patches.
                WinSpec text: 'rebuilds: ' , WinToolWindow rebuildCount printString
                        id: #rebuilds.
                WinSpec text: 'a click should move the first, not the second.'
                        id: #countsHint
            } id: #countCard
        } id: #root)
    ]

    "── the handler ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

     State in, rerender out. It never touches a control: what is on screen is
     a function of the state, and saying so once is what makes the difference
     between this and the Mac's editors, where every handler pokes its own
     widget and the two can disagree."

    WinDemoTool class >> handle: event <Symbol> value: value <Object> [
        event == #bump ifTrue: [ Clicks := self clicks + 1 ].
        event == #reset ifTrue: [ Clicks := 0. Note := nil. Loud := false ].
        event == #note ifTrue: [ Note := value ].
        event == #loud ifTrue: [ Loud := value ].
        ^self
    ]

    "── registration ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Declared at load time; it costs a dictionary entry until somebody opens
     the Tools menu."

    WinDemoTool class >> install [
        WinToolWindow
            register: #demo title: 'Declarative Demo' width: 460 height: 640
            render: [ WinDemoTool spec ]
            handler: [ :event :value |
                WinDemoTool handle: event value: value.
                (WinToolWindow named: #demo) rerender ].
        ^self
    ]
]

WinDemoTool install.
